WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
Australia has one of the highest melanoma rates

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:03.800
in the world.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:07.440
In 1981, a Victorian campaign told people to

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:10.800
slip, slop, slap, and in 1988 it grew

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:11.840
into SunSmart.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.040
Today, melanoma rates in younger Australians are falling.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.080
But was that the campaign, or something else?

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.720
So how can prevention campaigns reduce disease, and

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.240
how do we know one worked?

00:00:25.467 --> 00:00:28.666
You've seen how UV damages DNA in skin

00:00:28.747 --> 00:00:29.466
cells.

00:00:29.707 --> 00:00:33.226
Prevention means acting before disease or harm develops.

00:00:33.627 --> 00:00:36.986
Primary prevention stops the exposure itself, like sun

00:00:37.067 --> 00:00:37.626
protection.

00:00:38.027 --> 00:00:42.186
Secondary prevention catches disease early, before symptoms, like

00:00:42.267 --> 00:00:42.826
screening.

00:00:43.147 --> 00:00:46.106
Tertiary prevention limits the damage once disease is

00:00:46.187 --> 00:00:47.186
established.

00:00:47.467 --> 00:00:50.186
A campaign is a planned program of messages

00:00:50.267 --> 00:00:52.746
and actions, aimed at a defined group, to

00:00:52.907 --> 00:00:54.346
change one behaviour.

00:00:55.567 --> 00:00:58.446
Good campaigns start from the mechanism.

00:00:58.527 --> 00:01:01.486
For melanoma, the risk is UV reaching skin

00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:04.526
cells, so the target behaviour is reducing that

00:01:04.647 --> 00:01:08.206
exposure, especially in childhood, when sunburn adds the

00:01:08.367 --> 00:01:09.566
most risk.

00:01:09.807 --> 00:01:12.446
Then choose the audience, and decide before you

00:01:12.687 --> 00:01:14.259
start what you'll measure.

00:01:14.314 --> 00:01:16.606
If you can't name the exposure, the people

00:01:16.727 --> 00:01:19.059
and the measure, it's an advert, not a

00:01:19.087 --> 00:01:19.806
strategy.

00:01:21.034 --> 00:01:23.113
Here's the problem campaigns run into.

00:01:23.594 --> 00:01:26.233
People learn the message, agree with it, and

00:01:26.394 --> 00:01:30.953
still don't change, because habit, cost, convenience and

00:01:31.034 --> 00:01:34.033
what others do matter more than information.

00:01:34.394 --> 00:01:36.673
So SunSmart didn't rely on ads.

00:01:36.874 --> 00:01:40.073
Schools adopted hat and shade policies, councils built

00:01:40.154 --> 00:01:43.433
shade, UV alerts went into weather forecasts, and

00:01:43.514 --> 00:01:45.753
commercial solariums were banned.

00:01:46.074 --> 00:01:48.873
These change the conditions, so the safe choice

00:01:49.034 --> 00:01:50.233
becomes the easy one.

00:01:50.874 --> 00:01:53.673
Tobacco control worked the same way, with taxes

00:01:53.754 --> 00:01:56.393
and smoke-free laws alongside the ads.

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.840
So how do you measure whether it worked?

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:03.600
Process measures count what the campaign did: ads

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:05.440
shown, schools signed up.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.000
On their own, they can't tell you whether

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:08.920
it worked.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:12.160
Impact measures track the behaviour or exposure it

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:16.360
targeted: hats worn, sunscreen used, sunburn.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.640
Outcome measures track the disease itself, melanoma rates.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.240
Outcomes matter most, but melanoma takes decades to

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:27.440
appear, so early on, impact is the best

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:28.560
evidence you can get.

00:02:29.767 --> 00:02:33.526
SunSmart has impact evidence over thirty years.

00:02:33.687 --> 00:02:36.966
Summer surveys in Melbourne, repeated from 1987 to

00:02:37.047 --> 00:02:40.806
2017, found more people wearing hats and sunscreen

00:02:40.887 --> 00:02:43.846
and seeking shade, and fewer getting sunburnt on

00:02:43.927 --> 00:02:44.966
weekends.

00:02:45.167 --> 00:02:47.606
Another analysis found that in summers with more

00:02:47.687 --> 00:02:51.766
SunSmart TV advertising, sunburn was less common.

00:02:51.807 --> 00:02:56.086
That's like a dose-response: more campaign, more protection.

00:02:56.167 --> 00:02:58.326
It points to the campaign, not just a

00:02:58.487 --> 00:02:59.446
change in fashion.

00:03:00.667 --> 00:03:02.106
Now outcomes.

00:03:02.347 --> 00:03:05.786
In Queensland, invasive melanoma rates in people under

00:03:05.867 --> 00:03:10.666
forty fell between 1995 and 2014, and national

00:03:10.827 --> 00:03:12.746
rates in young adults have fallen since the

00:03:12.827 --> 00:03:14.026
late 1990s.

00:03:14.667 --> 00:03:17.386
These are rates, not counts, so a growing

00:03:17.467 --> 00:03:19.786
population can't hide the fall.

00:03:20.187 --> 00:03:22.986
In older Australians, rates kept rising.

00:03:23.467 --> 00:03:26.506
That fits latency: older people built up their

00:03:26.587 --> 00:03:29.706
UV damage before the campaign began, while younger

00:03:29.787 --> 00:03:31.706
people grew up with hats and shade.

00:03:32.187 --> 00:03:34.186
It's the pattern you'd expect if the campaign

00:03:34.267 --> 00:03:34.826
worked.

00:03:36.034 --> 00:03:37.873
So how sure can we be?

00:03:37.954 --> 00:03:41.953
Judge the secondary sources first, data someone else

00:03:42.034 --> 00:03:45.633
collected: who produced them, when, is there real

00:03:45.714 --> 00:03:48.273
data, and do independent sources agree?

00:03:48.754 --> 00:03:51.793
Here, they're health agencies and peer-reviewed studies that

00:03:51.874 --> 00:03:52.513
agree.

00:03:52.754 --> 00:03:55.553
But this national trend has no randomised control

00:03:55.634 --> 00:03:55.953
group.

00:03:56.354 --> 00:03:59.793
Behaviour was self-reported, and other exposures and policies

00:03:59.874 --> 00:04:00.673
changed too.

00:04:01.154 --> 00:04:03.313
So the evidence is strong, but it supports

00:04:03.354 --> 00:04:05.633
a package, not the ads alone.

00:04:06.834 --> 00:04:10.833
Effectiveness also means equity: whether the benefit reaches

00:04:10.914 --> 00:04:12.673
the people at highest risk.

00:04:13.154 --> 00:04:15.713
A campaign can lift the average while missing

00:04:15.834 --> 00:04:16.273
them.

00:04:16.594 --> 00:04:19.073
Teenagers and young adults still report the most

00:04:19.154 --> 00:04:22.113
sunburn, and outdoor workers are exposed on most

00:04:22.194 --> 00:04:23.273
working days.

00:04:23.554 --> 00:04:25.953
An improvement would reach them where they are,

00:04:26.034 --> 00:04:28.513
with shade, shirts and hats supplied at work

00:04:28.554 --> 00:04:32.193
and sport, so cost doesn't decide who's protected,

00:04:32.274 --> 00:04:34.993
and sunburn measured separately for those groups.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:37.640
Two exam questions.

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:38.680
First.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:42.360
A school built shade sails and added broad-brimmed

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:43.720
hats to its uniform.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:47.560
Over one school year, independent observers counted more

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:50.760
students in shade at lunch, and UV meters

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:53.560
worn by some students recorded 40 per cent

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:54.600
less exposure.

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:57.960
Evaluate this strategy for preventing melanoma.

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:00.040
Pause here to answer.

00:05:10.467 --> 00:05:13.426
Shade and a uniform hat protect students without

00:05:13.507 --> 00:05:14.786
relying on memory.

00:05:15.107 --> 00:05:17.346
More students in shade and 40 per cent

00:05:17.427 --> 00:05:20.546
less UV are impact measures, and they're stronger

00:05:20.627 --> 00:05:24.146
than self-report, because they were observed and measured.

00:05:24.227 --> 00:05:25.986
But one year can't show a fall in

00:05:26.067 --> 00:05:28.546
melanoma, which takes decades.

00:05:28.667 --> 00:05:31.346
So it's promising, with outcome evidence still to

00:05:31.507 --> 00:05:32.226
come.

00:05:32.307 --> 00:05:34.786
The classic mistake is saying it has already

00:05:34.867 --> 00:05:36.066
prevented melanoma.

00:05:37.267 --> 00:05:38.226
Second.

00:05:38.307 --> 00:05:40.946
A council ran an online campaign about type

00:05:41.027 --> 00:05:42.626
2 diabetes.

00:05:42.787 --> 00:05:46.226
Afterwards, residents knew more about its risk factors,

00:05:46.307 --> 00:05:48.946
but physical activity didn't change.

00:05:49.027 --> 00:05:51.986
Many residents work long shifts, and few streets

00:05:52.067 --> 00:05:53.586
have footpaths.

00:05:53.707 --> 00:05:56.146
Propose two changes that would make the strategy

00:05:56.227 --> 00:05:58.786
more effective, and a way to measure its

00:05:59.107 --> 00:05:59.986
success.

00:06:00.067 --> 00:06:01.346
Pause here to answer.

00:06:11.767 --> 00:06:14.646
Change the conditions, not just the message.

00:06:15.127 --> 00:06:18.166
Build footpaths and safe routes, so activity fits

00:06:18.287 --> 00:06:20.006
into travel and shift work.

00:06:20.247 --> 00:06:23.126
Offer free risk checks where people already go,

00:06:23.247 --> 00:06:26.246
like workplaces and pharmacies, then support those at

00:06:26.327 --> 00:06:27.686
high risk early.

00:06:27.927 --> 00:06:31.526
Measure activity first, then new diabetes rates over

00:06:31.607 --> 00:06:33.966
several years, with the same testing in a

00:06:34.007 --> 00:06:35.206
comparison area.

00:06:35.447 --> 00:06:38.726
The classic mistake is proposing more information.

00:06:39.934 --> 00:06:41.973
So, for any campaign.

00:06:42.254 --> 00:06:44.653
Name the exposure it targets and the people

00:06:44.734 --> 00:06:46.013
it must reach.

00:06:46.174 --> 00:06:49.693
Then judge the evidence at three levels, process,

00:06:49.974 --> 00:06:53.773
impact and outcome, and ask who it missed.

00:06:53.934 --> 00:06:56.333
Then propose a change that removes a barrier

00:06:56.414 --> 00:06:58.093
for those people.

00:06:58.494 --> 00:07:01.373
Next, when genetics can help prevent disease.

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:04.040
Here are the key notes.

00:07:04.280 --> 00:07:06.200
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:07.080
to copy them.
